questions.starter_code:
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}

questions.answer:
Change `var` to `let`:

for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
// 0
// 1
// 2

`var` has one function-scoped binding shared by all three callbacks, and by the time they run the loop has finished with `i === 3`. `let` creates a fresh binding per iteration, so each arrow closes over its own `i`.

questions.starter_code:
function summarize() {
  var total = 0;
  if (true) {
    var bonus = 10;
    total = total + bonus;
  }
  console.log(total, bonus);
}

summarize();

questions.answer:
function summarize() {
  let total = 0;
  if (true) {
    const bonus = 10;
    total += bonus;
  }
  console.log(total);
}

summarize(); // 10

`bonus` is only needed inside the block, so it becomes a block-scoped `const`; `total` is reassigned, so it stays `let`. Logging `bonus` outside would now throw, which is the point — the leak is gone.

